Organization: Customer of Access One Pty Ltd, Melbourne, Australia
Newsgroups: comp.os.unix,comp.unix.admin,comp.unix.osf,comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.unix.osf.osf1


Brian Connelly wrote:

> Has anyone seen an ftp problem that only lets you send data oneway? I
> can't
> figure out if there is a problem with the system, or with ftp. If you
> try
> and transfer the file, it will write the file with 0 bytes and then
> time
> out in about 10 min.

Yes I have.

Are you getting any errors in syslog? Does the transfer start up, i.e.
do you see

      150 starting binary/ascii data transfer ???

Does it make any difference how long the filename is?


The problem I have seen involved FTP-ing from an AS400 (3v7) to a Unix
system. FTP started up, but the connection message (the one above) was
never seen. FTP-ing to another Unix system (same configuration) worked.
Tests showed filenames of certain lengths (irrespective of contents)
could not be FTP-ed and this depended on the IP address of the machines.
Weird but true. There is an AS400 patch.

This problem took a lot of low level sniffing to work out, and we were
able to determine which filenames (lengths) would or wouldn't be
transfered. The error occurred in calculating the CRC checksum for the
header, which was rejected (rightly) by the remote system.

An other place to look would be something like /etc/ftpaccess to see if
you have excluded any hosts (subnets).


The only real way to find out what is happening is to get some low level
traces of the session from both machines.


Adam




